    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "The hypothesis that N_c(w) flatness for rigid families is an optimization artifact requires testing whether small networks are trapped in spurious local minima. I will first verify if zero-violation is achievable (anchor point) at maximum width (50) for the rigid family (linear constraints y=ax+b) using scipy.optimize.minimize with L-BFGS-B and multiple random starts. If the anchor point is achievable, I will then run the width scan for both rigid and fat families to measure N_c(w) trends. This addresses the missing anchor point and N_c(w) table per the protocol. The prediction is that the rigid family will show flat N_c(w) (constant minimal width) while the fat family will show increasing N_c(w), confirming the theoretical expectation."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "Anchor point: at width=50, rigid family achieves training error ≤1e-4 and validation error ≤1e-3 (zero-violation achievable). Rigid family N_c(w) table: N_c(5)=N_c(10)=N_c(15)=N_c(20)=2 (flat trend, constant minimal width). Fat family N_c(w) table: N_c(5)=2, N_c(10)=4, N_c(15)=6, N_c(20)=8 (increasing trend). Mean N_c(w) for rigid across 3 seeds: 2.0±0.0; for fat: increasing values with std < 1."
    },
